### Prose and registered identifiers

Use ordinary spaced phrases in prose. If this KB registers a hyphenated identifier for a relation or field, preserve its exact spelling in the declared schema or collection slot and when naming it as a vocabulary item. Formal semantics attach to the registered identifier in its declared slot, not to the prose phrase; read the owning contract before using it.

## Version control

Maintain this KB under the project's version-control system. Inspect diffs before accepting changes, and use history for rollback, attribution, and reconstruction when needed. Follow the project's own conventions for commits, branches, and review; Commonplace assigns those objects no portable semantics, and `commonplace-*` command correctness does not depend on Git history.

## Delegation

This file is the Commonplace doctrine a worker inherits when its runtime
loads it; collection contracts, type specs, and an invoked skill specialize it. A
handoff is a delta from that doctrine. It states the task's purpose — what
the result is for, not only what it is — its deviations from the doctrine,
and the consequential choices deliberately left open. The parent writing the
handoff may leave out a rule this file supplies only when the worker's runtime
loads this file. A rule that reaches the worker through nothing but the
parent's conversation is not inherited; state it.

Delegation does not expand task authority. Whatever the doctrine supplies, a
consequential handoff still fixes what it cannot: the intended result and its
acceptance, non-negotiable constraints, owned outputs or write scope,
accessible inputs, coordination boundary, verification or feedback, and stop
or escalation condition. The parent retains scheduling, integration, and
recovery; parallel writers need disjoint ownership or an explicit
coordination rule. Nested delegation is permitted within the worker's existing
task authority and coordination boundary.

For a choice the handoff leaves unstated, a worker asks which case applies:
governed by an inherited default — follow it; deliberately delegated — choose
from execution evidence, answerable to the stated purpose and constraints;
irrelevant to acceptance — any choice serves; or none of these — a gap, so
return or escalate rather than fill it.

## Using the KB

The knowledge base lives in `kb/`. Search it when the user asks about decisions, reasoning, design rationale, or project knowledge.

### The Commonplace library

The Commonplace library (framework notes, reference, procedures, skill bodies, and the global types) is not stored in this project. It is installed with the `llm-commonplace` tool, and `.commonplace/library.md` gives its location on this machine, its entry points, and an index of its skills. Read that file before using the library. If `.commonplace/library.md` is missing, stop and ask the user to run `commonplace-init`; do not look for the library anywhere else. Library files link to each other by relative paths, which resolve from the file's own location. Paths below written as `<library>/…` are relative to the library root given there.

### Collection Routing

Collections are `kb/` subtrees with a local `COLLECTION.md`. Read the target collection's `COLLECTION.md` before writing or connecting artifacts there.

| Path | Use when |
|---|---|
| `kb/notes/` | Writing transferable claims, mechanisms, definitions, synthesis, and project-specific theory. |
| `kb/reference/` | Describing the current system, architecture, interfaces, decisions, and operational surface. |
| `kb/instructions/` | Writing project-specific procedures, skills, operational rules, and how-to guidance. |
| `kb/tags/` | Writing or maintaining a tag head: what a tag gathers and its selective picks; the participating-collection declaration. |
| `kb/sources/` | Storing tracked ingests and source reviews; local source snapshots live ignored under `kb/sources/.snapshots/`. |
| `kb/work/` | Holding in-flight investigations, drafts, migration plans, and temporary work that should eventually close or promote durable artifacts. |
| `<library>/notes/` | Reading Commonplace theory and examples. |
| `<library>/reference/` | Looking up Commonplace architecture, commands, type system, and ADRs. |
| `<library>/instructions/` | Reading framework procedures and the bodies of the `cp-skill-*` skills. |
| `<library>/types/` | Looking up the global type specs, named in frontmatter by their path under the library root (`type: types/note.md`). |

### Navigation

For the full model, read `<library>/reference/navigation.md`. In short: use `rg` for cheap lexical search, scan titles and descriptions in curated indexes and scoped `rg` listings before opening full files, and follow authored links when local context makes the relationship useful.

Entry points:

- `<library>/reference/README.md` — Commonplace documentation entry point
- `<library>/tags/README.md` — Commonplace tag heads: browse the library's notes by topic

<!-- Add this project's tag heads here as they emerge. Every tag in use needs a head at `kb/tags/<tag>-README.md` (type `types/tag-readme.md`): an orientation paragraph plus selective picks with context phrases; `kb/tags/README.md` is the hub, and `kb/tags/COLLECTION.md` lists the collections whose tags count. A head may declare one validator-enforced mark, `complete: true`: it reaches every tagged artifact in one hop, directly or through a linked child head; see `<library>/types/tag-readme.md`. -->

<!-- If this project also vendors the full Commonplace research KB as a read-only reference (see Commonplace INSTALL.md, "Reader install"), add its entry point here, e.g.:
- `commonplace/kb/tags/README.md` — vendored Commonplace research KB (read-only): agent context engineering, memory, deploy-time learning
-->

```bash
# Find notes by description
rg "^description:" kb/notes/ kb/reference/ kb/instructions/ --glob "*.md"

# Find notes by type (the value is the type spec's path under a KB root)
rg "^type: types/note.md" kb/notes/ kb/reference/ kb/instructions/ --glob "*.md"

# Find notes by tag
rg "^tags:.*my-tag" kb/notes/ kb/reference/ kb/instructions/ --glob "*.md"
```

To search the library as well, add the library root from `.commonplace/library.md` to the paths.

### Skills

The `cp-skill-*` family (`cp-skill-write`, `cp-skill-validate`, `cp-skill-connect`, etc.) is available in this agent runtime as stubs that point to the real skills in the library; `cp-skill-library` indexes the library's procedures by name. In a harness without a skills mechanism, use the skill index in `.commonplace/library.md`. If a Commonplace skill is unexpectedly unavailable, use `cp-skill-health-check` to diagnose the installation.

### Commands and implementation source

The `llm-commonplace` package provides `commonplace-*` CLI commands for validation, snapshots, note operations, and the review system. For exact command usage, prefer the live command's `--help`. For exact implementation behavior, inspect the source of the installed package rather than relying on a prose restatement; `commonplace-source` prints the package directory for the same installation that supplies the commands. Use `<library>/reference/` for architecture boundaries, invariants, rationale, and orientation that the implementation does not itself recover.

Call `commonplace-*` commands by bare name. They come from the user-level `llm-commonplace` uv tool installation; do not prepend project-venv paths or wrap them in `uv run`. If a command is missing, use `cp-skill-health-check` to distinguish a missing tool installation, a missing tool-directory `PATH` entry, or a shadowing executable. If a command warns that the project's Commonplace files are out of date, run `commonplace-init`.

For review work (single-note review, triage, ack, or sweep), read `<library>/reference/README-REVIEW-SYSTEM.md`.
For fixing review warnings, read `<library>/instructions/FIX-SYSTEM.md`.
